<!--
  English function words (closed class) in LMF format.
  Same format as WordNet — processed by the same codegen functor.

  Sources (per category — closed-class enumerations are bounded
  references, not heuristic guesses). NOTE: this inventory is
  hand-curated and citation-anchored, NOT yet machine-loaded from the
  cited grammars (tracked praxis-debt, audit 2026-06-12):
    • POS taxonomy / category vocabulary:
        Chiarcos & Sukhareva (2015) "OLiA — Ontologies of Linguistic
        Annotation" *Semantic Web Journal* 6(4):379-386.
    • Adposition enumeration:
        Quirk, Greenbaum, Leech & Svartvik (1985) *A Comprehensive
        Grammar of the English Language* §9.1-9.7.
        Huddleston & Pullum (2002) *Cambridge Grammar of the English
        Language* Ch. 7.
    • Interrogative + possessive pronouns:
        Huddleston & Pullum (2002) Ch. 5 §5, §10.
    • Conjunction enumeration:
        Quirk et al. (1985) Ch. 13 (Coordination), Huddleston & Pullum
        (2002) Ch. 15 (Coordination and supplementation).
    • Determiner / copula / aux:
        Huddleston & Pullum (2002) Ch. 5 (determinatives) + Ch. 3
        (verbal categories).
    • Determiner definiteness/quantification feature (fw-*-det synsets):
        Lyons (1999) *Definiteness* (Cambridge); Abbott (2010)
        *Reference* (OUP) — see DeterminerKind.
    • Interjection communicative-function feature (fw-* synsets):
        Ameka (1992) "Interjections: the universal yet neglected part of
        speech" *J. Pragmatics* 18(2):101-118 — expressive / conative /
        phatic. (H&P 2002 Ch. 16 treats interjections as a class but
        gives no functional subclasses, so the kind is sourced here.)
    • Interrogative pronoun/determiner referent-type feature
      (fw-interrogative-{pron,det}-* synsets) and interrogative adverb
      role feature (fw-interrogative-adv-* synsets):
        Cysouw, M. (2004) "Interrogative words: an exercise in lexical
        typology", handout, session on question formation in Bantu,
        Bantu Grammar: Description and Theory 3, ZAS Berlin, 13 Feb 2004,
        §3.2 table (9) — who/whom/whose -> PERSON, what -> THING,
        which -> SELECTION, where -> PLACE, when -> TIME, how -> MANNER,
        why -> REASON. Building on Ultan, R. (1978) "Some general
        characteristics of interrogative systems", in J. Greenberg (ed.)
        *Universals of Human Language* vol. 4, Stanford UP, pp. 211-248
        (the cross-linguistic source Cysouw's own table cites). The OLiA
        `InterrogativeAdverb`/`InterrogativePronoun`/`InterrogativeDeterminer`
        subcat (above) covers all wh-words in each class uniformly; this
        is the finer feature that distinguishes them, riding the synset
        rather than the subcat — see WhReferentRole/WhAdverbRole.

  POS tags follow Universal Dependencies:
    det = determiner, pron = pronoun, adp = preposition,
    conj = conjunction, part = particle, cop = copula,
    aux = auxiliary, intj = interjection, num = numeral
-->
